@node D-Bus Bus Names
@section Bus Names and Activation

@subsection Dual bus name claim

cmacs claims two session-bus names every time @code{cmacs-dbus-start}
runs.  The dual claim mirrors emacsclient's daemon-vs-instance model
and solves two distinct discovery problems with one mechanism.

@subsubheading Well-known: @code{org.cmacs.Editor}

The first cmacs in a session to start the D-Bus service wins this
name.  Subsequent cmacs instances see it owned and run in
per-PID-only mode.  This is the name to target when you don't care
which cmacs you talk to --- desktop integration, file managers,
``Open With cmacs'' actions, AI agents, scripts.

The well-known name is also what @code{DBusActivatable} clients send
to, which is what triggers session-bus auto-activation when no cmacs
is running.

@subsubheading Per-PID: @code{org.cmacs.Editor.Pid@var{N}}

Every cmacs always claims this, where @var{N} is the process ID.
This gives you a stable, unambiguous handle on a specific instance,
so @command{cmacsgi} or a debugging script can target one cmacs out
of several running at once.

The per-PID name is published in the env var @env{CMACS_DBUS_NAME}
so child processes started by cmacs (vterm shells, @code{M-x bacon},
async shell commands) inherit it without having to re-discover.

@subsection Querying which name(s) you have

@example
(cmacs-dbus-name)             ;; "org.cmacs.Editor" if held, else
                              ;;    "org.cmacs.Editor.PidNNN"
(cmacs-dbus-well-known-name)  ;; "org.cmacs.Editor" or nil
(cmacs-dbus-per-pid-name)     ;; "org.cmacs.Editor.PidNNN"
@end example

From a shell:

@example
gdbus list --session | grep -E '^org\.cmacs\.Editor'
# org.cmacs.Editor
# org.cmacs.Editor.Pid12345
@end example

@subsection Picking which name to call

The well-known name is preferred for: ``open this file'', desktop
integration, one-shot queries.  The per-PID name is necessary for:
multi-instance disambiguation, debugging, tests.  A heuristic
wrapper:

@example
if gdbus list --session | grep -qx 'org.cmacs.Editor'; then
    DEST=org.cmacs.Editor
else
    DEST=$(gdbus list --session | grep -m1 '^org\.cmacs\.Editor\.Pid')
fi
gdbus call --session --dest "$DEST" \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
    --method org.cmacs.Editor1.Eval '(emacs-version)'
@end example

@subsection Service activation

The @file{/usr/share/dbus-1/services/org.cmacs.Editor.service} file
(or its per-user equivalent at
@file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/dbus-1/services/}) tells @command{dbus-daemon}
how to start cmacs on demand:

@example
[D-BUS Service]
Name=org.cmacs.Editor
Exec=/usr/bin/emacs --fg-daemon
SystemdService=cmacs.service
@end example

When any client sends a method call to @code{org.cmacs.Editor} and
no cmacs currently owns the name, @command{dbus-daemon}:

@enumerate
@item
Looks up the .service file by @code{Name=}
@item
If @code{SystemdService=} is present and a session systemd is
available, runs @command{systemctl --user start cmacs.service}
@item
Otherwise, exec()s the @code{Exec=} command line directly
@item
Queues the inbound message until the name is acquired (or a default
25-second timeout fires)
@end enumerate

@subsection Installing the activation file

@example
# System-wide (root):
sudo make install-cmacs-dbus-service

# Per-user (no sudo needed):
mkdir -p "$XDG_DATA_HOME/dbus-1/services"
sed "s|@@bindir@@|$HOME/source/projects/cmacs/src|g" \
    etc/dbus/org.cmacs.Editor.service.in \
    > "$XDG_DATA_HOME/dbus-1/services/org.cmacs.Editor.service"
@end example

The per-user form is handy for development: it lets you point the
activation file at your in-tree @file{./src/emacs} so any
``Open With cmacs'' click from a file manager runs your dev build.

@subsection Testing activation

After installing, kill any running cmacs and trigger activation:

@example
gdbus list --session | grep org.cmacs.Editor && echo "still running"

gdbus call --session \
    --dest org.cmacs.Editor \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
    --method org.cmacs.Editor1.Eval '(emacs-version)'
@end example

You should see cmacs spawn (check @command{ps aux | grep emacs}),
the bus name appear, and the eval result return.

@subsection Disabling auto-activation

Just remove the @file{.service} file:

@example
sudo make uninstall-cmacs-dbus-service
# or for per-user:
rm "$XDG_DATA_HOME/dbus-1/services/org.cmacs.Editor.service"
@end example

cmacs still works as a D-Bus service when manually started; it just
won't auto-launch.
